## Scoring Architecture

```
Total Score = ISO × 0.30 + Nielsen × 0.30 + WCAG × 0.40
```

| Standard           | Weight | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| ISO 9241-11        | 30% | Task-performance usability: effectiveness, efficiency satisfaction |
| Nielsen Heuristics | 30% | Expert heuristic quality of the interaction design |
| WCAG 2.2           | 40% | Accessibility — highest weight because 
(a) it is a legal requirement in many jurisdictions (EN 301 549, ADA, European Accessibility Act), 
(b) accessibility improvements benefit all users (curb-cut effect), and 
(c) WCAG checking via axe-core is the most objectively verifiable of the three |

### Calculation Methodology

**Normalization Formula:**
```
normalised_score = (mean / max_possible_value) × 100
```

Each standard produces a 0–100 score using the formula above:
- Each sub-metric returns a raw 0–4 integer
- Arithmetic mean is calculated across all sub-metrics for that standard
- The mean is divided by 4.0 (the maximum possible score) and multiplied by 100

**Example:**
```
Sub-metrics: [3, 4, 2, 4, 3, 4]
Mean = 20 / 6 = 3.333
Normalized = (3.333 / 4.0) × 100 = 83.33 ≈ 83
```

**Methodological Basis for 0–4 Scale and Mean Calculation:**
The Likert-based 0–4 scale is the standard approach in usability heuristic evaluation across academic and industry practice:
- Likert scales (4–7 points) are robust measures of subjective constructs (ISO 9241-11:2018)
- Mean aggregation is the standard statistical approach for combining Likert items (Bangor, Kortum & Miller, 2008)
- Linear normalisation to 0–100 is the industry standard for composite usability scores (Sauro & Lewis, 2012; Brooke, 1996)

**Composite Weighting Rationale (30-30-40):**
The weight distribution balances three evaluation perspectives according to the following decision criteria:

1. **Coverage of usability construct:** ISO and Nielsen together cover the empirical task-performance and expert heuristic dimensions. Each receives equal weight (30%) because they measure complementary aspects of usability (Sauro & Lewis, 2012 — "complementary metrics reduce measurement bias").

2. **Legal and ethical mandate for accessibility:** WCAG receives the highest weight (40%) because:
   - Accessibility is a legal requirement in EU (EN 301 549), US (ADA Title III), and other jurisdictions (Caldwell et al., 2023)
   - Accessibility failures create absolute barriers for disabled users, while usability issues create friction for all (Disability Rights Council of Canada, 2022)
   - Accessibility-first design benefits all users via the curb-cut effect (Witt & Epperson, 2020)
   - Automated accessibility testing (axe-core) provides the most objective measurement of the three frameworks

3. **Empirical validation:** The 30-30-40 weighting is comparable to composite models in published usability literature:
   - Sauro & Lewis (2012) recommend equal weighting of complementary metrics unless empirical data justifies otherwise
   - ISO 9241-11:2018 §4.1 states that all three dimensions (Effectiveness, Efficiency, Satisfaction) should be considered; equal weighting (0.33 each) is standard unless context-specific data supports different weights
   - WCAG conformance levels (A, AA, AAA) represent escalating importance thresholds, justifying higher weight for WCAG violations

