## ISO 9241-11 Metrics

**Standard:** ISO 9241-11:2018 *Ergonomics of human-system interaction — Part 11: Usability: Definitions and concepts.* Geneva: International Organization for Standardization.

ISO 9241-11 defines usability across three measurable dimensions: **Effectiveness**, **Efficiency**, and **Satisfaction**. The six functions each map to one of these dimensions.

---

### `nav_depth_score` → Effectiveness

**What it measures:** Navigation hierarchy depth (number of nesting levels inside `<nav>` elements).

**Why navigation depth = effectiveness?**
Effectiveness in ISO 9241-11 is defined as "the accuracy and completeness with which users achieve specified goals." Deep navigation menus require more clicks and increase working memory load, which directly reduces task completion rates — the primary indicator of effectiveness.

**Research basis:** Zaphiris & Mtei (1997) demonstrated experimentally that task errors increase significantly in hierarchical menus beyond two levels. Nielsen (2000, p. 198) recommends flat navigation of at most two levels for primary site sections. WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.5 (Multiple Ways, Level AA) independently recommends navigation structures that do not require deep traversal.

**Score mapping:**
| Max depth  | Score | Reason |
| ≤ 2        | 4     | Optimal — no submenu required |
| 3          | 2     | Acceptable — one sub-level |
| ≥ 4        | 0     | Exceeds recommendation |
| No `<nav>` | 2     | Neutral — page may not require navigation |

---

### `label_pairing_score` → Effectiveness

**What it measures:** Whether form inputs have programmatically associated labels.

**Why labels = effectiveness?**
Without a visible, associated label a user cannot accurately determine what data a field expects — directly failing the "accuracy and completeness" criterion of ISO effectiveness. This is also a testable WCAG 2.2 criterion (SC 1.3.1 Level A, SC 3.3.2 Level A).

**Why four detection patterns?**
HTML and ARIA define four equivalent label association mechanisms. The original code only checked `<label for="id">`, which would incorrectly penalise correct ARIA-based labelling. ISO 9241-171:2008 §9.3.14 requires *any* visible label in a consistent position — not specifically the `for=` pattern.

| Pattern | Spec |
| `<label for="id">` | HTML5 — canonical |
| `aria-label` on element | ARIA 1.2 |
| `aria-labelledby` referencing another element | ARIA 1.2 |
| Input nested inside `<label>` | HTML5 implicit label |

**Why include `<textarea>` and `<select>`?**
The original specification omitted these. ISO 9241-143:2012 §9.3 requires labels for all data-entry controls — not just `<input>` elements.

---

### `form_completion_score` → Efficiency

**What it measures:** Whether text-entry inputs have format hints (placeholder, title, aria-describedby).

**Why hints = efficiency?**
Efficiency in ISO 9241-11 is defined as "resources expended in relation to the accuracy and completeness with which users achieve goals." Format hints reduce the time and mental effort users spend working out what to type — a direct efficiency gain.

**Standard basis:** ISO 9241-143:2012 §7.2 explicitly recommends providing example values or format hints for any field where the expected format is not obvious. Nielsen (1994) Heuristic H6 (Recognition over Recall) supports this.

**Note on placeholders:** Placeholder text is not a substitute for a visible label (WCAG 2.2 Failure F65). This metric scores guidance quality independently of `label_pairing_score`.

---

### `heading_hierarchy_score` → Efficiency

**What it measures:** Whether HTML heading levels follow a sequential order without skipping levels (h1→h2→h3, no h1→h3 jumps).

**Why heading order = efficiency?**
A logical heading sequence allows users to rapidly scan and locate content without reading full body text — reducing time-on-task, which is efficiency. Skipping levels breaks the document outline for both sighted users (visual hierarchy collapses) and screen-reader users (who navigate by heading level).

**Standard basis:** WCAG 2.2 SC 1.3.1 (Level A) requires that heading markup conveys structural relationships. WebAIM's semantic structure guidelines explicitly prohibit skipping heading levels.

**Score mapping:**
| Skipped levels (jumps) | Score |
|---|---|
| 0 | 4 |
| 1 | 3 |
| 2 | 2 |
| 3+ | 0 |
| No headings | 2 (neutral) |

---

### `tab_order_score` → Efficiency

**What it measures:** Checks that interactive elements don't have positive tabindex values (tabindex="1", "2" etc.) which break natural keyboard navigation order.

**Why tab order = efficiency?**
Efficiency in ISO 9241-11 is defined as "resources expended in relation to the accuracy and completeness with which users achieve goals." Positive tabindex values disrupt the expected keyboard navigation order, forcing users to expend extra mental effort and time to navigate the page — a direct efficiency failure.

**Standards basis:** WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.3 Focus Order (Level A) explicitly prohibits positive tabindex values that disrupt logical tab order. WAI-ARIA 1.2 tabindex guidance.

**Scoring logic:** Penalty-based scoring where each positive tabindex reduces the score. 0 violations = 4, 1 = 3, 2 = 2, 3 = 1, 4+ = 0.

---

### `button_clarity_score` → Satisfaction

**What it measures:** Whether button labels use specific, non-vague action text.

**Why label clarity = satisfaction?**
Satisfaction in ISO 9241-11 includes "freedom from discomfort." Vague button labels ("OK", "Submit", "Click here") require users to infer what will happen — this inference work increases cognitive load and is a recognised source of frustration.

**Standard basis:** ISO 9241-143:2012 §8.4 specifies that command button labels should use specific action verbs describing the outcome ("Save order", "Send message"). Krug (2014) *Don't Make Me Think* Chapter 3: "Obvious always wins."

**Improvement over original:** The original ignored icon-only buttons with a descriptive `aria-label` (which are perfectly valid). The revised version also uses the `value` attribute as a fallback for `<input type="submit">`.

