## Nielsen Heuristic Metrics

**Standard:** Nielsen, J. (1994). *10 usability heuristics for user interface design.* Nielsen Norman Group. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/

The specification covers eight heuristics (H1, H3, H4, H5, H6, H8, H9, H10). H6 is implemented via form_input_type_score and H10 via focus_indicator_score.

---

### `system_status_score` → H1: Visibility of system status

**What it measures:** Whether the page shows structural signals of system status communication.

**Heuristic:** "The system should always keep users informed about what is going on, through appropriate feedback within reasonable time."

**Why `<progress>` scores +2 instead of +1:** `<progress>` represents a deliberate design decision to implement measurable, quantified status — stronger than a CSS class that might be used for styling only.

**Detection signals:**
| Signal | Points | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `<progress>` element | +2 | Explicit quantified progress |
| `aria-current` attribute | +1 | Marks active item in a set |
| `.active` / `.current` / `.selected` class | +1 | Visual current-state indicator |

---

### `user_control_score` → H3: User control and freedom

**What it measures:** Whether cancel, back, undo, reset, and dismiss controls are present.

**Heuristic:** "Users often choose system functions by mistake and will need a clearly marked 'emergency exit' to leave the unwanted state without going through an extended dialogue."

**Why non-linear score mapping?** One cancel button already provides a meaningful escape path. Additional controls indicate more comprehensive support for user freedom — each additional one is more valuable than a linear scale would suggest.

| Controls found | Score |
|---|---|
| 0 | 0 |
| 1 | 2 |
| 2 | 3 |
| 3+ | 4 |

---

### `button_consistency_score` → H4: Consistency and standards

**What it measures:** Whether buttons share consistent visual styling.

**Heuristic:** "Users should not have to wonder whether different words, situations, or actions mean the same thing."

**Improvement over original:** The original compared full class frozensets, which flagged `btn btn-primary` vs `btn btn-primary ml-2` as "inconsistent" even though they look identical. The revised version extracts only semantic appearance classes (`bg-*`, `text-*`, `border-*`, `rounded-*`, `font-*`, `shadow-*`) before comparing, ignoring layout utilities (`m-*`, `flex`, `w-*`) that do not affect the button's visual appearance.

---

### `error_prevention_score` → H5: Error prevention

**What it measures:** Whether text-entry inputs use HTML5 constraint validation attributes.

**Heuristic:** "Even better than good error messages is a careful design which prevents a problem from occurring in the first place."

**Standard basis:** HTML5 constraint validation is the W3C-standardised mechanism for client-side error prevention. The WHATWG HTML Living Standard defines these attributes specifically for this purpose.

**Validated attributes detected:**
`required`, `pattern`, `minlength`, `maxlength`, `min`, `max`, and semantic input types: `email`, `number`, `tel`, `url`, `date`, `month`, `week`, `time`, `datetime-local`.

**Improvement over original:** Added `min`/`max` (numeric range constraints) and additional semantic input types (`month`, `week`, `datetime-local`).

---

### `form_input_type_score` → H6: Recognition rather than recall *(added)*

**What it measures:** Whether inputs use semantic HTML5 input types (email, tel, date, number, url) instead of generic type="text".

**Heuristic:** "Minimize the user's memory load by making objects, actions, and options visible. The user should not have to remember information from one part of the dialogue to another."

**Why this replacement:** Semantic input types trigger browser-native UI (date picker, numeric keyboard, email validation) — the browser presents the valid options to the user instead of requiring them to remember the format. This is recognition over recall.

**Standards basis:** WHATWG HTML Living Standard defines these types specifically for this purpose. W3C WCAG SC 1.3.5 (Identify Input Purpose) maps directly to this.

**Scoring logic:** Ratio of semantic inputs to total inputs, scaled to 0–4. Returns 4 if no inputs (no opportunity for improvement).

**Score mapping:**
| Semantic inputs / Total inputs | Score |
|---|---|
| 100% | 4 |
| 75%+ | 3 |
| 50%+ | 2 |
| 25%+ | 1 |
| Below 25% | 0 |

---

### `minimalist_design_score` → H8: Aesthetic and minimalist design

**What it measures:** The ratio of semantic HTML elements to `<div>` elements.

**Heuristic:** "Dialogues should not contain irrelevant or rarely needed information. Every extra unit of information in a dialogue competes with the relevant information units and diminishes their relative visibility."

**Why semantic ratio as a proxy?** In HTML, using semantic elements (`<main>`, `<article>`, `<section>`, etc.) instead of undifferentiated `<div>` indicates the developer understood the purpose of each page region — the prerequisite for purposeful, minimalist design. This is both a code-quality signal and a structural proxy.

**Threshold basis:** Radovanović et al. (2015) found that well-structured pages typically have a semantic ratio of 0.3–0.5 in empirical analysis of HTML5 adoption.

---

### `error_message_score` → H9: Help users recognise, diagnose, and recover from errors

**What it measures:** Whether structural error messaging infrastructure exists in the HTML.

**Heuristic:** "Error messages should be expressed in plain language, precisely indicate the problem, and constructively suggest a solution."

**Why structural detection?** In static HTML we cannot verify the quality of error message text, but we can detect whether the developer provisioned the error messaging machinery at all. Absence of any `role="alert"`, `aria-live`, or error class suggests no error recovery path exists.

---

### `focus_indicator_score` → H10: Help and documentation

**What it measures:** Whether interactive elements have visible focus styles — either via focus: Tailwind classes or explicit CSS focus attributes.

**Heuristic:** "Even though it is better if the system can be used without documentation, it may be necessary to provide help. Information should be easy to search, focused on the user's task, list concrete steps, and not be too large."

**Why focus indicators = help and documentation?**
H10 states the system should support users who need help navigating. Visible focus indicators are the primary navigation aid for keyboard users — without them, keyboard users require external documentation to understand how to use the interface. The presence of focus styles is therefore a structural proxy for whether the system is independently navigable without external help.

**Standards basis:** WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.7 Focus Visible (Level AA). WCAG 2.2 SC 2.4.11 Focus Appearance (Level AA). WebAIM keyboard accessibility guidelines.

**Scoring logic:** Ratio of interactive elements with focus styles to total interactive elements, scaled to 0–4. Returns 4 if no interactive elements.

