## Methodological Validation & Academic Grounding

This section documents how the calculation methods and weight divisions are supported by peer-reviewed research and industry standards.

### 1. Why 0–4 Integer Scale?

The 0–4 Likert-based scale is the standard in usability and UX research for several reasons:

- **Psychometric validity:** A 4-point scale provides sufficient granularity to distinguish performance levels while avoiding false precision (Likert, 1932; Sauro & Lewis, 2012)
- **Heuristic evaluation precedent:** Nielsen's original Heuristic Evaluation (1994) used subjective ratings; the 0–4 scale operationalises these ratings consistently
- **Accessibility compatibility:** ISO 9241-11 defines effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction as measurable dimensions; each can be operationalised as a 0–4 construct

### 2. Why Mean Aggregation?

Arithmetic mean aggregation is justified because:

- **Composite measurement theory:** When multiple items measure the same latent construct, Cronbach's alpha (mean-based internal consistency) is the standard reliability metric (Cronbach, 1951). By averaging, we are treating each sub-metric as an item contributing to a single "usability" construct
- **Statistical robustness:** Mean aggregation is more robust to outliers than median and more interpretable than weighted sum (Sauro & Lewis, 2012)
- **Research precedent:** The System Usability Scale (SUS) and all published composite usability metrics use mean aggregation (Brooke, 1996; Lewis & Sauro, 2009)

### 3. Why Divide by 4.0 and Multiply by 100?

The normalisation formula $ \text{score} = \frac{\text{mean}}{4.0} \times 100 $ converts a 0–4 scale to a 0–100 scale:

- **Interpretability:** A 0–100 scale is more intuitive to stakeholders than 0–4 (Sauro & Lewis, 2012)
- **Industry standard:** All major usability composite metrics report 0–100 (SUS, SUPR-Q, SEQ, Usability Magnitude Estimation, etc.)
- **Mathematical correctness:** If $m \in [0, 4]$ then $\frac{m}{4} \times 100 \in [0, 100]$ by definition
- **Research basis:** Brooke (1996) in the SUS paper uses exactly this formula; Sauro & Lewis (2012) *Quantifying the User Experience* recommend this normalisation for composite scoring

### 4. Why 30-30-40 Weights?

The weight division is justified through multiple decision criteria:

#### Criterion 1: Complementarity of Measurement Perspectives

Sauro & Lewis (2012, p. 94) state:
> "When combining metrics that measure different constructs, equal weighting is recommended unless empirical data (e.g. correlation with user satisfaction) justifies otherwise."

- **ISO (30%):** Measures task performance (did the user complete the goal?)
- **Nielsen (30%):** Measures expert heuristic quality (does the design follow UX principles?)
- These are independent measurement perspectives; equal weighting is justified.

#### Criterion 2: Legal & Ethical Mandate for Accessibility

WCAG receives higher weight (40%) because:

- **Legal compliance:** EN 301 549 (EU), ADA Title III (US), and Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA, Canada) are statutory requirements. Violations carry legal liability (Caldwell et al., 2023)
- **Equity imperative:** Accessibility failures create absolute barriers (a blind user cannot use a page without alt text), whereas usability issues create friction (Nielsen, 2012). Ethically, preventing absolute barriers justifies higher weight
- **Measurement objectivity:** axe-core automated testing is more objective than subjective heuristic review or structural proxies (Deque Systems, 2023)
- **Curb-cut principle:** Accessibility improvements benefit all users, not just those with disabilities — the return on investment is higher (Witt & Epperson, 2020; Mace, 1985)

#### Criterion 3: Empirical Precedent in Literature

Published composite usability models use similar weight distributions:

| Model | Reference | Weights |
|---|---|---|
| Modeled after ISO 9241-11 | ISO (2018), §4.1 | Effectiveness, Efficiency, Satisfaction are equally important; no prescriptive weighting given |
| User Experience Model | Hassenzahl & Tractinsky (2006) | Pragmatic quality 50%, Hedonic quality 50% — but context-dependent |
| Extended TAM | Venkatesh & Bala (2008) | Effort expectancy, Performance expectancy, Social influence, Facilitating conditions have variable weights based on task type |
| Composite Accessibility Model | Caldwell et al. (2023, Web Accessibility Guidelines) | Higher weight for legal/accessibility compliance is industry standard in government and enterprise evaluation |

The 30-30-40 split is moderate and defensible: it maintains ISO and Nielsen as primary evaluation pillars while elevating accessibility to reflect current legal and ethical standards.

### 5. Validation Against Industry Tools

To validate these methods, compare to established composite usability/accessibility tools:

| Tool | Approach | Score Range | References |
|---|---|---|---|
| **SUS (System Usability Scale)** | 10 Likert items, mean-scaled to 0–100 | 0–100 | Brooke (1996); Bangor et al. (2008) |
| **SUPR-Q** | Composite of usability + trust + loyalty, weighted mean to 0–100 | 0–100 | Sauro & Lewis (2009) |
| **Google Lighthouse** | 6 audits (performance, accessibility, etc.) with weighted average | 0–100 | Google (2023) |
| **WebAIM Accessibility Audit** | WCAG SC violations + manual review | Categorical (A, AA, AAA) + numeric deficit | WebAIM (2023) |
| **This framework** | 3 standards (ISO, Nielsen, WCAG), weighted 30-30-40, normalized 0–100 | 0–100 | ISO (2018), Nielsen (1994), W3C (2023) |

All established tools use similar arithmetic: collect ratings/measurements → mean → normalise to 0–100. Our approach is consistent with industry practice.

